## Disk.validate rejects every NVMe session restore under the replay profile

Fixes the read side of the NVMe session tier: `Disk.validate` enforces two invariants that nothing else in the engine maintains, so under `TF_DSV41_PREFILL=replay` **every** disk restore is rejected with `BadBlob` and the prompt pays a full re-prefill. Reported as [jayleaton/deepseek-v41-tensorfold-spark#86](https://github.com/jayleaton/deepseek-v41-tensorfold-spark/issues/86).

### The two false invariants

**1. the stash span bound.** `forward_types.zig` documents what a stash holds:

```zig
/// positions [max(from, hi - keep), hi), ids.
pub fn lo(s: *const Stash) u64 { return @max(s.from, s.hi -| keep); }   // keep = 127
```

`extend()` never clamps `from`, and the writer agrees (`kv/sess.zig`: `stashRows(pos, from) = pos -| @max(from, pos -| keep)`). So `hi - from` may be arbitrarily large, while `ced.ring` is a compile-time `128` sized for the `ids` array only. This file is the only place in the tree requiring that bound, and it rejects the normal case: a prompt ingested as one segment (`from=0`, `hi=pos`).

**2. the `ds_rings` coupling.** `Sessions.init` gives a slot drafter rings only when `!replay and shape.ds_blocks > 0`, but `Sessions.snapshot` writes `ds` whenever `measure.ds_blocks > 0`, and `sessions_gpu.zig` consumes it exactly in that mode (`s.ds_valid[cur()] = m.pos - m.ds`, read by `validStart()` when `ds_rings` is false). Forbidding `ds != 0` without rings rejects the only value this mode produces.

Both were confirmed by serving an instrumented build of `20ad77878` and reading the failing operands on a live TP2 pair:

```
stash check: from=0 hi=59776 pos=59776 span=59776 ring=128
ds check:    ds=128 pos=59616 window=128 ds_rings=0
```

### Why the test suite is green here

`zig build test` at this pin: 5506/5537 pass, 29 skipped, 2 failed — both failures are qwen4 bf16-ULP logit ties in `test-cuda-host`, unrelated to sessions and present before and after this change. No session test fails: they ingest short segments, so `hi - from` never exceeds 128 and the `replay` × `ds_blocks > 0` combination never occurs.

### The change

Three lines, all making the validator agree with the type and with the writer — no check is deleted, and `!s.measure.replay and (from|hi != 0)` is untouched:

```diff
-    if (m.stash.from > m.stash.hi or m.stash.hi > pos or m.stash.hi - m.stash.from > ced.ring) return error.BadBlob;
+    if (m.stash.from > m.stash.hi or m.stash.hi > pos) return error.BadBlob;
-    var at = m.stash.from;
+    var at = @max(m.stash.from, m.stash.hi -| ced.keep);
-    if (m.ds > pos or m.ds > s.measure.window or (!s.ds_rings and m.ds != 0)) return error.BadBlob;
+    if (m.ds > pos or m.ds > s.measure.window) return error.BadBlob;
```

The ids loop now walks the held window instead of the whole logical range — previously it ran ~59.8k iterations across a 128-slot ring, i.e. it validated rows a recycled slot no longer holds.

### Measured on a 2×GB10 TP2 pair (production profile, 1024 MiB RAM tier)

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| `resumed … from NVMe` | 0 | 9 |
| `BadBlob` | every restore | 0 |
| recall TTFT, ~319k-token conversations | 155–178 s (own cold prefill) | 1.2–1.4 s |

Answers after an NVMe restore are byte-identical to the cold full prefill, including a codeword buried deep in the prefix; `from RAM` was counted separately in the same run, so these came off the disk tier.

### Not covered by this change

- non-replay profiles (where `ds_rings` is on) — the two dropped conjuncts are only ever violated under replay, so we expect no effect there, but that is inference, not a measurement;
- the alternative remedy — clamping `from` in `Stash.extend()` on the writer side — is equally defensible; we picked the reader because `lo()` and `stashRows()` already define the window by `keep`, and `extend()` not clamping `from` reads as deliberate.

(cherry picked from commit f1402df753a0e2b890b396037d07570686ac10e3)

